BCC_Q({"y": 2023, "n": 2, "part": "A", "topic": "code", "title": "Ogham Code", "answer": 0, "story": "<p>Ogham is a medieval alphabet used to write words vertically along a\npillar.<\/p>\n<p>Each letter is represented by a group of lines that always touches or\ncrosses the pillar in the same way. Groups (letters) are separated by\nbig gaps and arranged upwards from the bottom of the pillar to the top\nof the pillar.<\/p>\n<p>Eabha writes four words using the Ogham alphabet as shown. The words\nare BANANAS, BERRIES, LETTUCE, and ORANGES, but we do not know which\nword corresponds to which image.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"A description of the diagram follows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:100.0%\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc0')\" style=\"box-shadow: none;\">Hide/Reveal Description of Diagram for Ogham Code<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc0\" style=\"display: none;\">\n<p>Four pillars, each with seven groups of lines. For each pillar, from\nleft to right, the seven groups of lines are described in a list\nstarting with the bottom group (1) and ending with the top group (7).\nFor each group of lines, the following information is given: the number\nof lines in the group (one, two, three, four, or five), the direction of\nthe lines (horizontal or diagonal) and the location of the lines (to the\nleft of the pillar, to the right of the pillar, or crossing the\npillar).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Word 1<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>two, horizontal, to the right<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>three, horizontal, to the left<\/li>\n<li>three, horizontal, to the left<\/li>\n<li>three, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, to the left<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Word 2<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>two, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>five, diagonal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>one, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>five, horizontal, to the right<\/li>\n<li>two, diagonal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, to the right<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Word 3<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>one, horizontal, to the right<\/li>\n<li>one, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>five, horizontal, to the right<\/li>\n<li>one, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>five, horizontal, to the right<\/li>\n<li>one, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, to the right<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Word 4<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>one, horizontal, to the right<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>five, diagonal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>five, diagonal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>five, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, crossing<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, to the right<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/><\/div>", "question": "<p>From left to right, what is the order in which Eabha has written\nthese words?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li>LETTUCE, ORANGES, BANANAS, BERRIES<\/li>\n<li>ORANGES, LETTUCE, BANANAS, BERRIES<\/li>\n<li>BERRIES, BANANAS, LETTUCE, ORANGES<\/li>\n<li>LETTUCE, ORANGES, BERRIES, BANANAS<\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(A) LETTUCE, ORANGES, BANANAS, BERRIES<\/p>", "explanation": "<p>We will number the words in the image 1 to 4 from left to right.<\/p>\n<p>Word 3 and Word 4 begin with the same letter, so Word 3 and Word 4\nmust be BANANAS and BERRIES in some order. The 2nd, 4th, and 6th letters\nof Word 3 are all the same, so this makes Word 3 BANANAS and Word 4\nBERRIES.<\/p>\n<p>This teaches us the code for the letters in those two words, which we\ncan use to fill in some of the letters from Word 1 and Word 2 as\nwell:<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"A description of the diagram follows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:80.0%\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc1')\" style=\"box-shadow: none;\">Hide/Reveal Description of Diagram for Ogham Code\nExplanation<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc1\" style=\"display: none;\">\n<p>The groups of lines in Word 3 are labelled with the letters in\nBERRIES in order from bottom to top. The groups of lines in Word 4 are\nlabelled with the letters in BANANAS from bottom to top. Some of the\ngroups of lines in Words 1 and 2 are labelled with the letters from\nBERRIES and BANANAS as described below.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Word 1<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>two, horizontal, to the right: no label<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, crossing: labelled E<\/li>\n<li>three, horizontal, to the left: no label<\/li>\n<li>three, horizontal, to the left: no label<\/li>\n<li>three, horizontal, crossing: no label<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, to the left: no label<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, crossing: labelled E<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Word 2<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>two, horizontal, crossing: no label<\/li>\n<li>five, diagonal, crossing: labelled R<\/li>\n<li>one, horizontal, crossing: labelled A<\/li>\n<li>five, horizontal, to the right: labelled N<\/li>\n<li>two, diagonal, crossing: no label<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, crossing: labelled E<\/li>\n<li>four, horizontal, to the right: labelled S<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/><\/div>\n<p>Now it becomes clear that Word 2 must be ORANGES and Word 1 must be\nLETTUCE:<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:80.0%\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>Therefore, from left to right, Eabha wrote the words in the order\nLETTUCE, ORANGES, BANANAS, BERRIES.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>This task focusses on the idea of <em>encoding<\/em> information in a\nway to keep the information secret. One method of keeping data secret so\nthat unauthorized persons cannot access it is <em>encryption<\/em>, which\nis the key area of study in <em>cryptography<\/em>. Cryptography began as\nearly as 3500 years ago, with simple methods of encryption such as\nreplacing each letter in the message with a different letter, which is\nknown as a <em>substitution cipher<\/em>. Simple substitution ciphers can\nbe easily <em>decrypted<\/em> by using <em>frequency analysis<\/em>: some\nletters, such as \"E\" and \"T\" appear much more frequently than other\nletters, such as \"Q\" or \"Z\" in English text. Therefore, someone reading\na long encrypted message can try to guess that the most frequently\noccurring encrypted letters are probably \"E\" or \"T\" and begin to decrypt\nthe message. The decryption in this task is based on the common\noccurring letters between words.<\/p>\n<p>An alternative method to a direct substitution cipher is to use\n<em>rotation-based<\/em> ciphers, which typically were mechanical devices\nwith various rotors that had sequences of letters, and with each letter,\nthe rotor would move some amount. The most well-known of these sorts of\ndevices is the <em>Enigma machine<\/em> used to transmit messages during\nWorld War II. Similar to this machine in this task, each letter rotates\na different amount, such that the sequence of letters \"PA\" and \"SA\"\n(51-31 and 61-21) have the letter \"A\" encoded with two different values.\nThis type of encryption method is much more difficult to decrypt without\nknowing the original starting point of the arrow, and is much less\nvulnerable to frequency analysis.<\/p>\n<p>Many computer systems, such as <em>databases<\/em> and <em>web\nbrowsers<\/em>, rely on strong encryption methods to prevent unauthorized\naccess to information.<\/p>", "mc": 1});